id: rule-assign-two-variables
title: Two variables
topic: Assignment
example-of: ASSIGN
derived: true

task:
  Use the rule ASSIGN to decide whether this triple can be derived. The
  names $x'$ and $y'$ are logical variables: the program never assigns
  them, and they keep the initial values of $x$ and $y$.

given:
  Assignment expression: $y + x$

pre: y = y' && x = x'
post: y = y' + x' && x = x'

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  y := y + x     // i1
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The substitution
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i1
text:
  The precondition is obtained by substituting the variable $y$ with the
  assignment expression $y + x$ in the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(y := y + x, Q)
  = (y = y' + x' && x = x')[y <- y + x]     // Q
set: R1 = (y = y' + x' && x = x')[y <- y + x]

step: Apply the substitution
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i1
text:
  Only $y$ is replaced. The variables $x$, $x'$ and $y'$ are other
  variables, so they stay as they are.
calc:
  (y = y' + x' && x = x')[y <- y + x]
  = y + x = y' + x' && x = x'      // y replaced by y + x
set: R1 = y + x = y' + x' && x = x'

step: Use the second conjunct
rule: LOGIC
focus: R1
text:
  The second conjunct says $x = x'$, so in the first conjunct we can
  replace $x'$ by $x$.
calc:
  y + x = y' + x' && x = x'
  = y + x = y' + x && x = x'       // x' replaced by x, using x = x'
set: R1 = y + x = y' + x && x = x'

step: Simplify
rule: LOGIC
focus: R1
text:
  We subtract $x$ from both sides of the first conjunct.
calc:
  y + x = y' + x && x = x'
  = y = y' && x = x'               // subtract x from both sides
set: R1 = y = y' && x = x'

step: P implies R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  The precondition $P$ is the formula we obtained.
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: y = y' && x = x' ==> y = y' && x = x'
why:
  The two formulas are the same, so the rule ASSIGN gives the triple, with
  the consequence rule for the simplified form.
